Microprocessor Unit (MC6800)

##TOP

    .. rubric:: Comparison: Motorola MC6800L2 vs. MC68B00

    The processing cores and performance characteristics of the **MC6800L2** and the 
    **MC68B00** are identical; both are rated for a maximum clock speed of 2.0 MHz. 
    However, they represent separate eras in Motorola's packaging technology and 
    part-numbering conventions.

    .. rubric:: Part Number Architectural Breakdown


    .. rubric:: MC6800L2 (Early 2.0 MHz Designation)
    
    * **MC**: Motorola Integrated Circuit Prefix
    * **6800**: Core 8-bit architecture reference
    * **L**: Ceramic Dual In-line Package (Ceramic DIP)
    * **2**: Factory-binned performance tier certifying stable operation at 2.0 MHz

    .. rubric:: MC68B00 (Standardized Family Designation)
    
    * **MC**: Motorola Integrated Circuit Prefix
    * **68**: Architectural family group
    * **B**: Integrated speed alphanumeric identifier (explicitly indicating 2.0 MHz)
    * **00**: Base CPU core configuration
    * **Package Suffix**: Typically omitted in broad documentation, but stamped on hardware as **P** (Plastic) or **S** (Cerdip)


    .. rubric:: Physical and Generational Divergence

    .. table:: Structural and Materials Comparison Matrix
            :widths: 25 35 40

        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+
        | Feature           | MC6800L2                   | MC68B00                    |
        +===================+============================+============================+
        | **Production Era**| Mid-to-late 1970s          | Late 1970s through 1980s   |
        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+
        | **Max Clock Rate**| 2.0 MHz                    | 2.0 MHz                    |
        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+
        | **Enclosure**     | Premium Ceramic base with  | Black epoxy plastic (P) or |
        |                   | gold-plated lid            | glass-sealed cerdip (S)    |
        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+
        | **Pin Metallurgy**| Gold-plated                | Tin-plated                 |
        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+
        | **Availability**  | Rare vintage collector tier| High volume surplus        |
        +-------------------+----------------------------+----------------------------+

    .. rubric:: Hardware Interoperability Guidelines

    .. note::
        From an electrical and execution standpoint, these two microprocessors are 
        fully interchangeable drop-in replacements. Both utilize identical 40-pin 
        DIP layouts and require matching external two-phase non-overlapping clock 
        driver circuitry.

    .. rubric:: Timing Equivalence Rule

    Both components share identical internal bus cycle metrics:

    .. math::

        f_{max} = 2.0\text{ MHz} \implies t_{cyc} \ge 500\text{ ns}

    The minimum allowable clock pulse high width (:math:`PWH_{\phi\_1}`) for both 
    variants is strictly bounded:

    .. math::

        PW_{H} \ge 180\text{ ns}

    .. warning::
        While a ceramic **MC6800L2** can always replace a plastic **MC68B00P**, replacing an MC6800L2 with a plastic MC68B00P in high-temperature or high-humidity industrial settings may degrade environmental tolerance due to the shift from gold/ceramic to tin/plastic materials.

